    technicalOverview:
      'Inconel 625 gains its strength from molybdenum and niobium dissolved in a nickel-chromium matrix. Solid-solution hardening does the work: no ageing treatment is needed for a grade 1 annealed forging to certify at 827 MPa minimum tensile strength in sections up to 4 in. (102 mm). Nickel sits at a 58.0% minimum and chromium at 20.0-23.0%, a chemistry that resists pitting, crevice attack and stress corrosion in seawater, brines and mixed acid streams from cryogenic temperatures upward. European drawings name the identical material W.Nr. 2.4856 or NiCr22Mo9Nb. Production range covers standard flange sizes from NPS 1/2" to 24" in ASME B16.5 Classes 150 to 2500. High-pressure requirements (Class 1500 and 2500) above NPS 24 are fully non-standard and manufactured directly to custom customer design calculations.',
